# Facile fabrication of CdS-metal-organic framework nanocomposites with enhanced visible-light photocatalytic activity for organic transformation

Fei ${\mathsf{K}}{\mathsf{e}}^{1,2}$ , Luhuan Wang1, and Junfa Zhu1 $(\boxtimes)$
1 National Synchrotron Radiation Laboratory and Collaborative Innovation Center of Suzhou Nano Science and Technology, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230029, China   
2 Department of Applied Chemistry, Anhui Agricultural University, Hefei 230036, China
$\circleddash$ Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2014
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, porous materials, nanocomposites, photocatalysis, semiconductors
## ABSTRACT
Visible-light-initiated organic transformations have received much attention because of low cost, relative safety, and environmental friendliness. In this work, we report on a novel type of visible-light-driven photocatalysts, namely, porous nanocomposites of CdS-nanoparticle-decorated metal-organic frameworks (MOF), prepared by a simple solvothermal method in which porous MIL-100(Fe) served as the support and cadmium acetate $(\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{Ac})_{2})$ as the CdS precursor. When the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde is used as the probe reaction, the results show that the combination of MIL-100(Fe) and CdS semiconductor can remarkably enhance the photocatalytic efficiency at room temperature, as compared to that of pure CdS. The enhanced photocatalytic performance can be attributed to the combined effects of enhanced light absorption, more efficient separation of photogenerated electron–hole pairs, and increased surface area of CdS due to the presence of MIL-100(Fe). This work demonstrates that MOF-based composite materials hold great promise for applications in the field of solar-energy conversion into chemical energy.
## 1 Introduction
With the rapid industrial development, environmental pollution and energy shortages have led to an awareness of a potential global crisis [1]. For this reason, the development of both pollution-free technologies for environmental remediation and alternative clean energy supplies has become an urgent task. In the last few years, visible-light photocatalysis has emerged as a powerful tool in synthetic organic chemistry due to its natural abundance, ease of use, environmental friendliness, and fascinating potential applications [2–7]. Semiconductor nanoparticles (NPs) with high photocatalytic activity and strong quantum-size effect are considered to be promising photocatalytic materials [8–13]. To date, most semiconductor materials are based on transition-metal oxides, sulfides, or nitrides with suitable band gaps and band positions [14]. Among them, crystalline cadmium sulfide (CdS) has been extensively studied because of its narrow band gap $(2.4\mathrm{eV})$ , which corresponds well to the visible spectral range of solar radiation, its size-dependent optical and electronic properties [15, 16], and diverse applications such as solar cells [17], environmental purification [18], hydrogen evolution [19], and chemical sensors [20]. However, there are still several issues that limit the utilization of pure CdS NPs, including their aggregation in reactions, high recombination rate of photogenerated electron–hole pairs, and separation/ reuse of NPs from photoreactors [21]. Therefore, it remains an important challenge to improve the performance of CdS photocatalysts. Over the years, a number of technologies have been explored to enhance the photocatalytic activity of CdS NPs, such as doping with noble metals [22], synthesis of CdS quantum dots (QDs) [23], and combination with other semiconductors [24] or carbon materials [25]. However, there is still plenty of room to explore unfulfilled potentials for improving the fabrication and properties of CdS-based photocatalysts.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as porous coordination polymers (PCPs), are highly ordered, nanoporous networks that are derived from metallic centers bonded by terminal organic linkers [26]. MOFs have attracted a great deal of attention in both academia and industry due to their numerous potential applications in the fields of gas storage [27], sensing [28], drug delivery [29], and catalysis [30]. In addition to these attractive applications, we are particularly interested in utilizing MOFs as active structures for light absorption and excited-state applications in energy-conversion processes [31]. If a MOF is employed as light-sensitive semiconductor, the central metal-oxide cluster can be considered as a discrete QD, which is stabilized and interconnected by the organic linkers acting as photon antenna [32]. Recently, a few important studies demonstrating the promise of MOFs in the conversion of solar radiation to electrical or chemical energy have been reported [33]. For example, the so-called MOF-5, also known as IRMOF-1, has an absorption spectrum with onset at $450\mathrm{nm}$ and can undergo photochemical processes upon photoexcitation of the organic linker [34]. In 2007, Llabrés i Xamena et al. [35] first reported on the photocatalytic activity of MOF-5 in the degradation reaction of phenols. Later, Kent and co-workers [36] reported on the fabrication and energy-migration dynamics of metal-bipyridine-based MOFs for light harvesting. Further, Long et al. [37] reported on an amine-functionalized zirconium MOF (UiO-66-NH2) used as visible-light photocatalyst for the selective aerobic oxygenation of various organic compounds. However, the photoactivity of this type of MOF was not as effective as that of inorganic semiconductor NPs. Compared with traditional semiconductor photocatalysts, the superiority of MOFs originates from their ultrahigh surface area and narrow micropore distribution, which may lead to the formation of monodisperse photoactive species supported on MOFs [38].
Considering that both semiconductor NPs and MOFs have their own advantages and disadvantages if used as photocatalysts, if one combines them, such semiconductor–MOF hybrid materials are expected to possess advanced properties originating from the two different components and to overcome their individual shortages mentioned above. To date, there have been a few pioneering studies on semiconductor– MOF hybrid structures for enhanced light harvesting (e.g., CdSe/ZnS–MOF [39] and GaN@ZIF-8 [40]) or size-selective molecular sensing applications (e.g., CdTe/ZIF-8 [41] and CdSe/CdS/ZnS@MOF-5 [42]). However, the utilization of semiconductor–MOF hybrid structures as photocatalysts in organic synthesis has rarely been investigated. Only very recently, Shen et al. [43] first reported on the application of $\mathrm{CdS\mathrm{-UiO-}}66(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ nanocomposites synthesized by a photodeposition technique for the photocatalytic oxidation of alcohols. Unfortunately, the photocatalytic activity is relatively low due to the large size of the used CdS nanorods.
Herein, we report on a novel CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposite that shows promising application potential in the photocatalytic oxidation of benzyl alcohol under ambient conditions. A series of CdS– MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites with different weight percentages of added MIL-100(Fe) have been successfully synthesized by a facile solvothermal method, as illustrated in Scheme 1. The MIL-100(Fe) supporting matrix remarkably improves the photostability and photocatalytic efficiency of the CdS photocatalyst for the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde. Therefore, the presently reported CdS–MIL100(Fe) nanocomposites are more suitable as photocatalysts for large-scale applications in industry.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc})$ was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich Co. LLC. Ferric trichloride hexahydrate, cadmium acetate, dimethyl sulfoxide, nitric acid, and hydrofluoric acid were purchased from Sinopharm (Shanghai) Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China. All other chemicals used in this work were of analytical grade, obtained from commercial suppliers, and used without further purification unless otherwise noted.
## 2.2 Synthesis of MIL-100(Fe)
According to the literature [44], MIL-100(Fe) was prepared by reacting H3btc $(0.8466\mathrm{g})$ with $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(1.6263\mathrm{g})$ in the presence of aqueous HF $(0.213\mathrm{mL})$ , ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ $(0.163\mathrm{mL})$ ), and distilled water $(30~\mathrm{mL})$ ) at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . Then, the orange solid was recovered by filtration, washed with hot water and ethanol, and finally dried overnight at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum.
## 2.3 Synthesis of CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites
Up to $240\mathrm{mg}$ of as-synthesized MIL-100(Fe) was dispersed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) by ultrasonication to obtain the homogeneous MIL-100(Fe)–DMSO dispersion. Then, $0.106\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Cd(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ was added to the above solution. After vigorous stirring, the mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave $(50\mathrm{mL})$ ) and reacted at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . After cooling, the obtained products were collected by centrifugation, washed with acetone and ethanol, and finally dried overnight at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum. For control purpose, pure CdS was also synthesized in DMSO under the same reaction conditions as those of CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites, except for the addition of MIL-100(Fe).
## 2.4 Photocatalytic tests
The photocatalytic activity of the samples was evaluated by the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde under visible-light irradiation provided by a 500 W Xe lamp with cutoff filter at $420\mathrm{nm}$ . In a typical process, $16\mathrm{mg}$ of as-synthesized photocatalyst was dispersed into $3\mathrm{mL}$ of toluene, and then $0.2\mathrm{mmol}$ of benzyl alcohol was added. Here, we chose toluene as solvent for the catalysis reaction. Although a previous study showed that the conversion rate of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde using benzotrifluoride as solvent is slightly higher than in the case of using toluene $42\%$ vs. $38\%$ ) [21], the price of benzotrifluoride is much higher than that of toluene. Prior to irradiation, the mixture was stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ to ensure homogeneity of the solution containing the photocatalyst blend. After reaction, the mixture was centrifuged at $12,000~\mathrm{rpm}$ to completely remove the photocatalyst particles. The remaining solution was analyzed by gas chromatography (GC) and the products were identified by comparison with authentic samples. The conversion of benzyl alcohol (BA) and selectivity for benzaldehyde (BAD) were defined as follows
Conversion $(\%)=\left[\left(C_{0}-C_{\mathrm{BA}}\right)/\:C_{0}\right]\times100$
## 3 Results and discussion
where $C_{0}$ is the initial concentration of ${\mathrm{BA}},$ and $C_{\mathrm{BA}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{BAD}}$ are the concentrations of BA and BAD, respectively, at a certain time after the photocatalytic reaction.
## 2.5 Characterization
The morphologies of the samples were observed by using a JEM-2100F transmission electron microscope at $200\mathrm{kV}.$ The porosity properties of the catalysts were characterized by nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ on a Micromeritics TriStar II 3020 adsorption analyzer. The powder $x$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the samples were collected using an $x$ -ray diffractometer with $\mathtt{C u}$ target $(36\mathrm{kV},25\mathrm{mA})$ ) from $3^{\circ}$ to $65^{\circ}$ . The UV–Vis diffuse reflectance spectra were recorded at room temperature on a Shimadzu UV-2550 spectrophotometer using $\mathrm{BaSO_{4}}$ as reference. The electrochemical measurements were carried out in a conventional three-electrode cell using a Pt plate and an $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ electrode as counter and reference electrode, respectively. The working electrode was prepared on indium-tin oxide (ITO) glass, which was cleaned by sonication in ethanol for $20\mathrm{min}$ and dried at $353\mathrm{K}$ . The $\scriptstyle10-{\mathrm{mg}}$ sample was dispersed in $1\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol by sonication to get a slurry. The slurry was spread onto the pretreated ITO glass whose boundary was previously protected using scotch tape. After air drying, the working electrode was further dried at $393\mathrm{K}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ to improve adhesion. Then, the scotch tape was unstuck and the working electrode was obtained for further measurements. The electrolyte was $0.5{\mathrm{}}\mathrm{M}{}$ aqueous $\mathrm{Na}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4}$ solution without additive. The photoluminescence (PL) spectra of the solid samples were investigated on a Hitachi F-4500 fluorescence spectrophotometer. The radical species were detected by electron spin resonance (ESR) spectrometry on a JES-FA200 instrument. The conversion percentage and purity of the final products were determined using a GC1690 gas chromatograph equipped with a flame ionization detector (FID) and high-purity helium as carrier gas. The products were identified by comparison with authentic samples.
A series of samples were synthesized by adding different amounts of MIL-100(Fe). Specifically, 0, 7.5, 15, 30, 60, 120, and $240\mathrm{mg}$ of MIL-100(Fe) were used, and the samples were named as CdS, CdS–MIL100(Fe)-7.5, CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-15, CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-30, CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60, CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-120, and CdS– MIL-100(Fe)-240, respectively. The crystal structures and phase purities of the as-synthesized samples were examined by PXRD. Figure 1 shows the typical PXRD patterns of CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites synthesized with different contents of MIL-100(Fe). For comparison, the patterns of pure CdS and MIL100(Fe), as well as that of simulated MIL-100(Fe) are also shown. As evident, all diffraction peaks of the CdS–MIL-100(Fe) samples can be indexed according to the hawleyite CdS phase with lattice constant $a=$ 5.818 Å (JCPDS No. 10-0454) [23] and cubic crystalline MIL-100(Fe) [45], and no peak assigned to any impurities is detected. Moreover, with decreasing the amount of MIL-100(Fe) in the samples, the intensities of the CdS reflections become more intense. Accordingly, the peak intensities of the MIL-100(Fe) patterns become weakened, implying that the CdS content in the nanocomposites gradually increases. The CdS diffraction peaks are broad due to the small crystallite size of the CdS NPs $\leq5\mathrm{nm},$ calculated by the Scherrer formula using the (111) diffraction peak) in the samples, resulting from the slow release of $S^{2-}$ ions from DMSO, which serves as solvent and source of sulfur during the formation of CdS NPs [19].
Porosities and Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas of the as-synthesized samples were examined by nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. S3 in the ESM). It is obvious that the introduction of MIL-100(Fe) clearly affects the BET surface area of the samples. Compared with pure CdS, the CdS– MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites exhibit a significant increase in the amount of adsorbed nitrogen. As shown in Table 1, the BET surface area $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of the samples gradually increases with increasing MIL-100(Fe) content, from 66.1 to $1,536.2\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . It is expected that larger surface areas of photocatalysts can supply more surface-active sites and make charge-carrier transport easier, in turn leading to enhanced photocatalytic performance [19, 43]. Therefore, MIL-100(Fe) may play a vital role in enhancing the photocatalytic activity.
CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-120, and CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-240 nanocomposites, respectively. This indicates that the introduction of MIL-100(Fe) to form CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites can narrow the band gap of the semiconductor CdS. This may arise from the chemical bonding between CdS and the MIL-100(Fe) support. Similar phenomena were also observed for other graphene-based semiconductor photocatalysts [21, 24]. If the amount of MIL-100(Fe) in the nanocomposites becomes too high (CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60, CdS–MIL100(Fe)-120, and CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-240), the absorbance drops, reflecting increased band-gap values of CdS in these highly doped CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites. This can be ascribed to the fact that the high weight ratio of MIL-100(Fe) to CdS results in increased diffuse reflectivity and deteriorates the light-absorption ability of the nanocomposites [25].
Given its favorable structure, the photocatalytic activity has been evaluated by the model reaction of the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde under visible-light irradiation. As shown in Fig. 4, when comparing the overall activities of the different photocatalytic samples with the same total amount of the photocatalyst $(16~\mathrm{mg})$ , an interesting trend is found. For pure CdS $\mathrm{NPs,}$ as expected, a relatively low photocatalytic activity $(40\%)$ is observed due to the rapid recombination of photogenerated electron–hole pairs [19]. If a small amount of MIL-100(Fe) is added, compared with pure CdS, the CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites exhibit enhanced photocatalytic performance with respect to the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde, considering both the degree of conversion of benzyl alcohol and selectivity to benzaldehyde (Table 1). In particular, when the CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposite was used in this reaction, the optimal photocatalytic performance is obtained, for which the conversion level reaches the highest value of $54.3\%$ among all investigated samples and, notably, the selectivity to benzaldehyde is nearly $100\%$ . However, it is worth to mention that the gain in photocatalytic activity achieved by the CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposite is only a factor of 1.4 compared to that of pure CdS NPs. At first glance, one may argue that this improvement is not significant, so that the photocatalytic activity of the catalysts maybe entirely attributed to the presence of CdS particles. However, we should keep in mind that this activity value was measured based on the same total catalyst amount, namely $16~\mathrm{mg}$ (Table 1). In fact, the actual content of CdS in the CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposite is only $7.84\mathrm{mg},$ corresponding to only $49\%$ of pure CdS used. If we scale up to the same amount of CdS used (or if the same amount of CdS would have been used), the photocatalytic activity will be significantly higher after the introduction of porous MIL-100(Fe) to CdS NPs. A similar study reported previously on the photocatalytic activity of CdS–UiO-6 $6(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ catalysts [43], for which the conversion level of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde reached $31\%$ (in the solvent of benzotrifluoride for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ ).
The further increase of the MIL-100(Fe) weight ratio in CdS–MIL-100(Fe) leads to the deterioration of their photocatalytic activity, which is also observed in other carbon or graphene-based CdS nanocomposites [19, 21, 24, 25]. Considering that the CdS NPs in the CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites (CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-30, CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60, CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-120, and CdS– MIL-100(Fe)-240) have similar dispersivity and almost the same size, the different conversion levels among these nanocomposites clearly demonstrate that MIL-100(Fe) plays a vital role in enhancing the photocatalytic activity. The introduction of the most appropriate amount of MIL-100(Fe) to the CdS NPs can remarkably improve the photocatalytic efficiency. Because porous MIL-100(Fe) can enhance the adsorption for benzyl alcohol, the benzyl-alcohol molecules can accumulate at the surface of the CdS NPs, thus resulting in the acceleration of the photocatalytic reactions. However, at high weight ratios of MIL-100(Fe) in the CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites, the effective surface of the semiconductor CdS NPs toward light irradiation is reduced and results in the rapid decrease of photogenerated charges, ultimately reducing the photocatalytic activity [21]. Thus, there should be an optimal weight ratio of MIL-100(Fe) in CdS–MIL100(Fe) nanocomposites. Moreover, in blank experiments without photocatalyst or light, the oxidation process was not observed. This result demonstrates that the photocatalyst and light irradiation are indispensable for the effective photocatalytic selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol.
Since the nanocomposite CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 shows the best catalytic performance regarding the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde under visible-light irradiation, we chose this material to evaluate the stability and reusability of CdS–MIL100(Fe) catalysts. After complete reaction, the photocatalysts were separated from the reaction solution by centrifugation and washed with ethanol and then dried at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . After this treatment, the photocatalysts were used again for further consecutive runs under the same reaction conditions. As shown in Fig. 5, no significant loss in the degree of conversion of benzyl alcohol can be observed in five successive photocatalytic cycles, indicating that the CdS–MIL-100(Fe) photocatalysts possess excellent long-term catalytic stability and reactivity.
In order to further check whether MIL-100(Fe) plays the key role in the photocatalytic selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde, we carried out additional comparative experiments using pure CdS NPs and CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposites under visible-light irradiation which was provided by a 500 W Xe lamp with a cutoff filter at $520~\mathrm{nm}$ . As can be seen from Fig. S5 in the ESM, under identical reaction conditions, pure CdS NPs only show a very low photocatalytic activity $(6.2\%)$ under visible-light irradiation $(\lambda>520\mathrm{nm})$ for $5\mathrm{h},$ while in the case of the CdS– MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposite, the conversion level reaches $15.4\%$ , being 2.5 times higher than that of pure CdS. Therefore, these results clearly demonstrate that MIL-100(Fe) plays an important role in the enhancement of the photocatalytic activity of the CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites.
To deepen the understanding of the role of MIL100(Fe) in the enhancement of the photoactivity of CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites, we have performed some further characterizations, including electrochemical analysis and PL spectroscopy. Figure S6 in the ESM exhibits Mott–Schottky plots of MIL-100(Fe). The negative slope of the $C^{-2}{-}E$ plot indicates that pure MIL-100(Fe) is a p-type semiconductor. The enhanced photocatalytic activity of the CdS–MIL100(Fe) nanocomposites, compared to pure CdS, can be ascribed to the formation of $\mathtt{p-n}$ junctions between p-type MIL-100(Fe) and n-type CdS semiconductor NPs. It can be inferred that the flat-band potential $E_{\mathrm{FB}}$ of p-type MIL-100(Fe) is around $1.15\mathrm{V}$ vs. $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ (equivalent to $1.35\mathrm{V}$ vs. the normal hydrogen electrode, NHE). The calculated conduction-band potential $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ is $-0.45\mathrm{V}$ vs. NHE. Because the calculated $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ of n-type CdS is $-0.52\mathrm{V}$ vs. NHE [21, 24], which is more negative than the $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ of MIL-100(Fe) $(-0.45\mathrm{V}$ vs. NHE), the photogenerated electrons could be transferred from the CB of CdS NPs to MIL-100(Fe) under visible-light irradiation. At the same time, the holes could be transferred from the valence band (VB) of MIL-100(Fe) to the CB of CdS NPs (Fig. 7, inset). Therefore, separation of photogenerated electronhole pairs is facilitated, which can be confirmed by the transient photocurrent response. Figure S7 in the ESM shows the transient photocurrent responses of the same amount of pure CdS and CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposites under intermittent visible-light irradiation. It can be clearly seen that the photocurrent of CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 is enhanced significantly compared to that of pure CdS. It is well known that the photocurrent is formed mainly by the diffusion of photogenerated electrons toward the back contact; meanwhile, the photoinduced holes are taken up by the hole acceptor in the electrolyte [21, 43]. Thus, the enhanced photocurrent obtained for CdS–MIL100(Fe)-60 can be attributed to the more efficient separation of photogenerated electron–hole pairs than in the case of pure CdS NPs. This is also confirmed by the results of PL spectroscopy. As shown in Fig. S8 in the ESM, the PL intensity of CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 appears much weaker than that of pure CdS, indicating that the recombination of photogenerated electron– hole pairs is hampered in the nanocomposite system.
The efficient separation of photogenerated electron– hole pairs in the CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposite is beneficial for photocatalytic processes and should contribute to its enhanced photocatalytic activity. Similar findings are also reported in previous researches [21, 24].
To determine further advantages of the CdS–MIL100(Fe) nanocomposites against pure CdS NPs, we have additionally performed electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and the results are presented in form of Nyquist plots. As shown in Fig. S9 in the ESM, the two Nyquist plots of pure CdS and CdS– MIL-100(Fe)-60 electrode material cycled in $0.5{\bf M}$ $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolyte solution likewise show semicycles at high frequencies. Considering the identical preparation conditions of the electrodes and electrolyte, the high-frequency semicircles are relevant to the resistance of the electrodes [21]. It is known that the high-frequency arc in Nyquist plots corresponds to the limiting charge-transfer process and can be attributed to the double-layer capacitance $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ in parallel with the charge-transfer resistance $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ at the contact interface between electrode and electrolyte solution [22, 24]. Notably, the introduction of MIL-100(Fe) leads to a dramatic decrease of the arc, compared to pure CdS, suggesting much more efficient charge transfer in CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposites than in pure CdS. Thus, the separation of photogenerated electron–hole pairs is facilitated significantly, which is consistent with the results of the transient photocurrent-response analysis.
Additionally, ESR analysis of the CdS–MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposites was carried out to study the involvement of active radicals in the photocatalytic process. As can be seen from Fig. 6, the characteristic peaks belong to superoxide radicals $(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet}-)$ but no nonselective hydroxyl radical signals can be detected in the CdS– MIL-100(Fe)-60 nanocomposite suspension using toluene as solvent under visible-light irradiation [22, 48]. No signals could be detected without irradiation. Because the standard oxygen-reduction potential $(-0.15\mathrm{V}$ vs. NHE) [49] is less negative than the CB edge of CdS (–0.52 V vs. NHE) [24], the formation of superoxide radicals is thermodynamically favorable. From these results, we can conclude that the formation of strongly oxidative hydroxyl radicals under such reaction conditions hardly takes place, thus the photogenerated hole and $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\bullet_{-}}$ can oxidize benzyl alcohol with high product selectivity. Based on these observations, we propose a simple mechanism displayed in Fig. 7 for the photocatalytic selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol assisted by the CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites: Under visible-light irradiation, electrons are excited from the VB of the CdS semiconductor to its CB, leaving behind holes in the VB. The photogenerated electrons of CdS can be transferred to MIL-100(Fe) owing to their intimate interfacial contact [24, 43]. According to the band-energy values (Fig. 7, inset), this process is thermodynamically permissible; then, the electrons react with adsorbed oxygen to form superoxide radicals whose existence has been confirmed by ESR. At the same time, adsorbed benzyl alcohol interacts with the holes to form corresponding radical cations, which further react with oxygen or superoxide radicals to given rise to the product of benzaldehyde [21, 22, 50]. As a result, the efficiency toward the photocatalytic redox process is improved.
On the basis of the results reported in the present work, the primary role of an appropriate amount of porous MIL-100(Fe) in CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites can be summarized as follows: (i) The amount of MIL-100(Fe) controls the resulting morphology of the CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites during the synthesis process. (ii) Visible-light absorption is enhanced. (iii) The separation of photogenerated electron–hole pairs is facilitated significantly. All these factors account for the observed enhanced photoactivity of CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites as compared to pure CdS with respect to the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde under visible-light irradiation. It is expected that our present work may provide useful information for the fabrication of other MOF-based semiconductor nanocomposites, as well as to open a new doorway for utilizing semiconductor– MOF nanocomposites in the field of visible-lightdriven photocatalysis in organic synthesis.
## 4 Conclusions
We have reported a facile solvothermal method to synthesize CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites with different weight ratios of MIL-100(Fe). In the synthesis process, porous MIL-100(Fe) can function as an excellent supporting matrix for the evenly distributed CdS NPs. The introduction of MIL-100(Fe) has been found to have a significant effect on the morphology, porosity, and optical properties of the CdS NPs. The resulting CdS–MIL-100(Fe) nanocomposites exhibit excellent photostability and enhanced photocatalytic activity with respect to the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde under visible-light irradiation. The improved photocatalytic activity can be ascribed to the combined effect of enhanced light absorption, more efficient separation of photogenerated electron– hole pairs, and larger surface area. The present results demonstrate the significant potential of scale-up synthesis of such CdS–MOF nanocomposites and the exploration of their properties in heterogeneous photocatalysis.
